Linear search is the simplest way to find a value: check every element, one by one, until you find it or run out of elements. It makes no assumptions about the data — it works on unsorted arrays just as well as sorted ones — which is exactly why it's slow: it can't skip anything.

```ts
function linearSearch(arr: number[], target: number): number {
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (arr[i] === target) {
      return i;
    }
  }
  return -1;
}
```

Worst case: the target is the last element (or missing entirely), so every element gets checked — O(n) time, O(1) extra space. It's the baseline every other search algorithm tries to beat.

Binary search finds a value in a **sorted** array by repeatedly cutting the search space in half: compare the target to the middle element, and discard the half that can't contain it. That single assumption — sortedness — is what makes it fast.

```ts
function binarySearch(arr: number[], target: number): number {
  let low = 0;
  let high = arr.length - 1;
  while (low <= high) {
    const mid = Math.floor((low + high) / 2);
    if (arr[mid] === target) {
      return mid;
    } else if (arr[mid] < target) {
      low = mid + 1;
    } else {
      high = mid - 1;
    }
  }
  return -1;
}
```

Each comparison eliminates half of the remaining candidates, giving O(log n) time — for a billion elements, at most ~30 comparisons.

Bubble sort repeatedly walks through the array, swapping any two adjacent elements that are out of order. Each full pass "bubbles" the largest remaining value to its final position at the end.

```ts
function bubbleSort(arr: number[]): number[] {
  const n = arr.length;
  for (let i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
      if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
        [arr[j], arr[j + 1]] = [arr[j + 1], arr[j]];
      }
    }
  }
  return arr;
}
```

It's O(n^2) in the average and worst case — simple to understand, but rarely used in practice. Its one redeeming feature: it's trivial to make it stop early once a full pass makes no swaps.

Selection sort splits the array into a sorted prefix and an unsorted suffix. On each pass it scans the unsorted suffix to find its minimum, then swaps that minimum into place at the front of the suffix.

```ts
function selectionSort(arr: number[]): number[] {
  const n = arr.length;
  for (let i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
    let minIndex = i;
    for (let j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
      if (arr[j] < arr[minIndex]) {
        minIndex = j;
      }
    }
    [arr[i], arr[minIndex]] = [arr[minIndex], arr[i]];
  }
  return arr;
}
```

Like bubble sort it's O(n^2), but it makes at most n - 1 swaps total (versus potentially O(n^2) swaps for bubble sort) — useful when writes are expensive.

Insertion sort builds a sorted prefix one element at a time: it takes the next unsorted element and shifts every larger element in the sorted prefix one step to the right, until it finds the right spot to insert it — the same technique people use to sort a hand of playing cards.

```ts
function insertionSort(arr: number[]): number[] {
  for (let i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const current = arr[i];
    let j = i - 1;
    while (j >= 0 && arr[j] > current) {
      arr[j + 1] = arr[j];
      j--;
    }
    arr[j + 1] = current;
  }
  return arr;
}
```

Worst case is O(n^2), same as bubble and selection sort — but its best case, on already-sorted input, is O(n), since the while loop never shifts anything. That adaptiveness makes it a good choice for small or nearly-sorted arrays.

Merge sort is a divide-and-conquer algorithm: split the array in half, recursively sort each half, then merge the two sorted halves back together. The merge step is the only place any real work happens — walk both halves with two pointers, always taking the smaller front element.

```ts
function mergeSort(arr: number[]): number[] {
  if (arr.length <= 1) {
    return arr;
  }
  const mid = Math.floor(arr.length / 2);
  const left = mergeSort(arr.slice(0, mid));
  const right = mergeSort(arr.slice(mid));
  return merge(left, right);
}

function merge(left: number[], right: number[]): number[] {
  const result: number[] = [];
  let i = 0;
  let j = 0;
  while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {
    if (left[i] <= right[j]) {
      result.push(left[i]);
      i++;
    } else {
      result.push(right[j]);
      j++;
    }
  }
  return [...result, ...left.slice(i), ...right.slice(j)];
}
```

Unlike the O(n^2) sorts above, merge sort guarantees O(n log n) in every case — best, average, and worst — at the cost of O(n) extra memory for the merged arrays.

Quicksort is also divide-and-conquer, but it splits differently: pick a pivot, partition the array so everything smaller than the pivot ends up to its left and everything larger ends up to its right, then recursively sort each side. Partitioning is done in place, which is why quicksort typically outperforms merge sort in practice despite having a worse worst case.

```ts
function quickSort(arr: number[], low = 0, high = arr.length - 1): number[] {
  if (low < high) {
    const pivotIndex = partition(arr, low, high);
    quickSort(arr, low, pivotIndex - 1);
    quickSort(arr, pivotIndex + 1, high);
  }
  return arr;
}

function partition(arr: number[], low: number, high: number): number {
  const pivot = arr[high];
  let i = low - 1;
  for (let j = low; j < high; j++) {
    if (arr[j] < pivot) {
      i++;
      [arr[i], arr[j]] = [arr[j], arr[i]];
    }
  }
  [arr[i + 1], arr[high]] = [arr[high], arr[i + 1]];
  return i + 1;
}
```

Average case is O(n log n), but a poor pivot choice (e.g. always picking the last element on already-sorted input) degrades it to O(n^2).

A singly linked list stores elements as a chain of nodes, where each node holds a value and a reference to the next node. Unlike an array, elements aren't stored contiguously in memory, so there's no indexing — reaching the k-th element means walking k links from the head.

```ts
class ListNode {
  value: number;
  next: ListNode | null = null;
  constructor(value: number) {
    this.value = value;
  }
}

class LinkedList {
  head: ListNode | null = null;

  append(value: number): void {
    const node = new ListNode(value);
    if (!this.head) {
      this.head = node;
      return;
    }
    let current = this.head;
    while (current.next !== null) {
      current = current.next;
    }
    current.next = node;
  }
}
```

The tradeoff versus an array: O(1) insertion at the head (no shifting needed), but O(n) access to an arbitrary element instead of an array's O(1).

A stack is a Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) structure: the most recently added item is the first one removed — like a stack of plates. It only exposes operations at one end, which is what makes it useful for things like undo history, expression evaluation, and function call tracking.

```ts
class Stack<T> {
  private items: T[] = [];

  push(item: T): void {
    this.items.push(item);
  }

  pop(): T | undefined {
    return this.items.pop();
  }

  peek(): T | undefined {
    return this.items[this.items.length - 1];
  }

  isEmpty(): boolean {
    return this.items.length === 0;
  }
}
```

All four operations run in O(1), because they only ever touch the end of the underlying array.

A queue is a First-In-First-Out (FIFO) structure: the first item added is the first one removed — like people waiting in line. It's the natural fit for anything processed in arrival order: task scheduling, breadth-first search, print jobs.

```ts
class Queue<T> {
  private items: T[] = [];

  enqueue(item: T): void {
    this.items.push(item);
  }

  dequeue(): T | undefined {
    return this.items.shift();
  }

  front(): T | undefined {
    return this.items[0];
  }

  isEmpty(): boolean {
    return this.items.length === 0;
  }
}
```

Note the asymmetry with Stack: enqueue adds at the back (push) but dequeue removes from the front (shift). Using a plain array, shift() is O(n) in the worst case — real-world queues use a circular buffer or linked list to keep every operation O(1).
